### Health check with REST Client
GET http://localhost:4000/api/health

### Search stations
GET http://localhost:4000/api/stations?search=par

### One station
GET http://localhost:4000/api/stations/parramatta

### Station not found (expect 404)
GET http://localhost:4000/api/stations/hogwarts

### Departures (soonest first)
GET http://localhost:4000/api/stations/parramatta/departures

### Alerts (severe first)
GET http://localhost:4000/api/alerts

### Reports for Parramatta (newest first)
GET http://localhost:4000/api/reports?stationId=parramatta

### Unknown API route (expect 404 JSON)
GET http://localhost:4000/api/trains
### Create a valid report (expect 201)
POST http://localhost:4000/api/reports
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "stationId": "parramatta",
  "category": "lift",
  "description": "  Lift on platform 2 not working since this morning  "
}

### Invalid report: 3 problems (expect 400)
POST http://localhost:4000/api/reports
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "stationId": "hogwarts",
  "category": "aliens",
  "description": "Broken"
}

### Sneaky report: tries to set its own status and id (expect 201, extras ignored)
POST http://localhost:4000/api/reports
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "stationId": "central",
  "category": "safety",
  "description": "Broken glass near the ticket gates",
  "status": "resolved",
  "id": 1
}

### Empty body (expect 400)
POST http://localhost:4000/api/reports
Content-Type: application/json

{}

/*
Format rules for POST in REST Client
//POST http://...                      ← line 1: method + URL
//Content-Type: application/json       ← line 2: header (tells Express "this is JSON")
                                     ← line 3: MUST be EMPTY
//{ ... }                              ← then the body

// Without the blank line, REST Client treats the body as more headers and the request breaks.
Without Content-Type: application/json, express.json() ignores the body, req.body stays empty, and you get a 400.
*/